# Strength and certainty of graded recommendations in ESICM, SCCM and ACP clinical practice guidelines, {{ mf.search.start[:4] }}–{{ mf.search.cutoff_year }}: an automated, reproducible analysis

*Draft generated from analysis package `{{ analysis.package }}`, snapshot {{ analysis.snapshot.id }}, extractor version `{{ analysis.extractor.version_id[:12] }}`. Do not edit numbers here; edit the template.*

## Abstract

**Background.** *(outline)* Strength of a recommendation and certainty of its evidence are distinct; how often critical care and internal medicine guidelines make strong recommendations on low-certainty evidence is not routinely tracked.

**Methods.** We searched PubMed from {{ mf.search.start }} to {{ mf.search.cutoff }} for clinical practice guidelines led or co-led by ESICM, SCCM or ACP and included {{ flow.included.all }} guidelines ({{ flow.current.all }} current editions) with at least one strength- and certainty-graded recommendation. A locally run language model ({{ mf.extractor.model_name }}) extracted each recommendation with its grade; grades were harmonized to GRADE strength, direction and certainty.

**Results.** Of {{ t.ALL.n_graded }} graded recommendations in current editions, {{ t.ALL.strong_pct | pct }}% were strong ({{ t.ALL.strong_num }}/{{ t.ALL.strong_den }}; 95% CI {{ t.ALL.strong_ci_low | ci(t.ALL.strong_ci_high) }}), {{ t.ALL.against_pct | pct }}% recommended against an intervention, and {{ t.ALL.high_moderate_pct | pct }}% rested on high or moderate certainty evidence ({{ t.ALL.high_moderate_num }}/{{ t.ALL.high_moderate_den }}). On {{ dev.n_guidelines }} labelled development guidelines, extraction reached graded F1 {{ dev.candidate.f1 | p3 }}; on {{ held.n_guidelines }} held-out SCCM guidelines, F1 {{ held.f1 | p3 }}.

**Conclusions.** *(outline)* Descriptive distributions only; feasibility and reproducibility of an updateable extraction pipeline.

## Introduction

*(outline)*

- Recommendation strength vs certainty of evidence (GRADE); why a strong recommendation on low certainty evidence deserves scrutiny.
- Prior work: Fanaroff et al., JAMA 2019 (ACC/AHA cardiology levels of evidence); earlier critical care level-of-evidence studies.
- Gap: manual audits are one-off; guidelines change. An updateable extraction pipeline makes the audit repeatable.
- Aim: describe strength, direction and certainty of graded recommendations in ESICM, SCCM and ACP guidelines; report extraction accuracy separately on development and held-out guidelines.

## Methods

### Search and selection

We ran {{ mf.search.queries | length }} PubMed queries ({{ (mf.search.queries | length) // (mf.corpus.included_by_society | length) }} per society; exact terms in supplement S1) for records first published online from {{ mf.search.start }} to {{ mf.search.cutoff }}, and cross-checked the three societies' guideline web pages. The search returned {{ flow.candidates.all }} candidate records{% for source, n in flow.source.items() %}{% if loop.first %} ({% endif %}{{ source }} {{ n }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% else %}){% endif %}{% endfor %}.

Eligible: a clinical practice guideline led or co-led by ESICM, SCCM or ACP, in any journal, with at least one recommendation graded for both strength and certainty; adult and paediatric guidelines and GRADE-based rapid guidelines included. Excluded formats: ACP rapid living practice points (certainty without strength), ACP guidance statements and best-practice advice, consensus documents, executive summaries and other derivative papers. A co-published guideline counts once, under the society of the retained publication; the other copies are duplicate publications. {{ mf.search.cutoff_year }} is a partial calendar year.

### Editions

Each guideline belongs to a topic; a newer edition supersedes an older one by an explicit link. Rule: {{ mf.corpus.edition_rule }}. Cross-sectional tables use the {{ flow.current.all }} current editions; time trends use all {{ flow.included.all }} editions. Focused updates that revise only some questions stay current beside the guideline they update, so revised questions count twice (an estimated {{ mf.corpus.focused_update_overlap_max }} graded recommendations at most).

### Extraction and harmonization

Extractor version `{{ mf.extractor.version_id }}`: model {{ mf.extractor.model_name }} (digest `{{ mf.extractor.model_digest[:12] }}`), single pass over the full PDF text. The raw strength and certainty strings are stored as extracted and harmonized on read; the complete observed mapping is in the supplement ({{ raw_harmonized_mapping | length }} distinct raw grades). A statement offering no direction is not a graded recommendation, whatever label the model attached. No grade is inferred when the label cannot be mapped.

### Outcomes and denominators

The unit is one extracted, deduplicated recommendation. Only graded recommendations enter the primary tables. Strong (%) and against (%) use graded recommendations with a mapped strength; high/moderate certainty (%) uses those with a mapped certainty. Unmapped grades are reported as counts, not imputed. ALL is pooled per recommendation, so it is dominated by SCCM.

### Uncertainty

95% intervals come from a guideline-cluster percentile bootstrap ({{ mf.bootstrap.n }} resamples, seed {{ mf.bootstrap.seed }}): guidelines are resampled within each society and, for ALL, within each society and pooled. The intervals describe between-guideline variability in this curated corpus; they do not correct extraction error or establish representativeness.

### Sensitivity analysis

Table S1 weights each guideline equally: the unweighted mean of each guideline's own percentage, against the pooled estimate.

### Extraction accuracy

Development: {{ dev.n_guidelines }} labelled ACP and ESICM guidelines, which also informed extractor development (in-sample). Held-out: {{ held.n_guidelines }} SCCM guidelines drawn at random before any result, labelled blind to the extractor output, scored with the same matching (BioLORD similarity, Hungarian assignment) and harmonization; not used for tuning or gating.

### Reproducibility

All numbers in this draft come from one frozen copy of snapshot {{ analysis.snapshot.id }} (SHA-256 `{{ analysis.inputs.database.sha256 }}`), rendered by one command without a language model. Analysis code `{{ analysis.code.analysis_sha }}`; checksums of every input and output are in `analysis_manifest.json`.

