## Discussion

*(outline)*

- Main findings: share of strong recommendations, share against, certainty distribution; strong recommendations on low or very low certainty evidence.
- Comparison with Fanaroff et al. (JAMA 2019) and earlier critical care level-of-evidence studies; differences among societies are descriptive, not causal.
- Strengths: full corpus of three societies, prespecified denominators, cluster intervals, frozen and reproducible package, separate held-out evaluation.
- Limitations: development-set optimism; held-out sample of {{ held.n_guidelines }} SCCM guidelines; imperfect precision (restatements, comparison tables); single-reviewer spot check; variable PDF layouts; ungraded statements not captured; residual grade-mapping uncertainty; focused-update overlap; curated corpus and search limits; partial {{ mf.search.cutoff_year }}.
- Next: ungraded-statement pass (v3); recommendation-level supersession; other specialties.

## Conclusion

*(outline)* Restricted to the observed distribution of graded recommendations and to feasibility; no claim that a strong recommendation on low certainty evidence implies inappropriate care.

